# Caddy Proxy Manager Environment Configuration
#
# This file is a REFERENCE to read, not a template to copy. A fresh install
# generates the only two values it needs straight into .env:
#
#   echo "SESSION_SECRET=$(openssl rand -base64 32)" >> .env
#   echo "POSTGRES_PASSWORD=$(openssl rand -base64 32)" >> .env
#   chmod 600 .env
#
# That is the whole file. Copying this one over it -- `cp .env.example .env`,
# how an install began before 3.0 -- still works, but only gets you a .env that
# is almost entirely commented-out lines duplicating what the setup page asks
# for anyway.
#
# Every line here is commented out, and that is deliberate: the install appends
# the two generated values, so a placeholder above them would only be a second,
# weaker assignment of the same name -- and the two that mattered were a
# password published in this repository. Uncomment a line only when you
# actually mean to set it.
#
# Nothing is lost by leaving a line commented. Every variable below either has
# a working default or is a Settings field you fill in from the browser, except
# SESSION_SECRET and POSTGRES_PASSWORD -- and Compose refuses to start without
# those, rather than starting on something guessable.
#
# Most settings now live in the DATABASE, not here. A fresh install needs only
# SESSION_SECRET and POSTGRES_PASSWORD; everything else is entered in the
# browser on first run and edited afterwards on the Settings page. Prefer that
# to setting them here: a secret saved in the app is encrypted at rest and is
# handed to `docker compose` in the environment of the command the agent runs,
# rather than sitting in plaintext on the host for as long as this file exists.
#
# Resolution order is: stored value -> this file -> built-in default. So a
# variable set here still works and still wins over the default, but is
# overridden once the same setting is saved in the app -- at which point it can
# be deleted from this file. Anything marked "Compose reads this" must stay
# regardless, because Docker cannot read the database.

# =============================================================================
# DATABASE (REQUIRED)
# =============================================================================

# PostgreSQL is the default backend (SQLite: see DATABASE_URL further down). Compose
# reads these to provision the bundled `postgres` service, so they stay here rather
# than moving to Settings.
# Generate the password with: openssl rand -base64 32
#
# Any password works as typed -- base64 output contains `/` and `+`, and the app
# is handed these as discrete fields rather than folded into a URL, so there is
# nothing to escape. See DATABASE_URL further down for the exception.
#POSTGRES_PASSWORD=your-postgres-password-here
#POSTGRES_USER=cpm
#POSTGRES_DB=cpm

# =============================================================================
# REQUIRED SECURITY SETTINGS (PRODUCTION)
# =============================================================================

# Session Secret (REQUIRED)
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
# Must be at least 32 characters in production.
#
# This is also the HKDF root every secret in the database is encrypted with --
# DNS provider credentials, imported private keys, agent secrets. It cannot live
# in the thing it encrypts. To rotate it, move the old value to
# SESSION_SECRET_PREVIOUS below; changing it without doing so makes them unreadable.
#SESSION_SECRET=your-secure-session-secret-here-min-32-chars

# Previous session secret(s), comma-separated, after rotating SESSION_SECRET.
# Only ever used to decrypt: each start re-encrypts whatever still needs one of
# them under SESSION_SECRET, so it can be removed after one start with it set.
# Signed-in sessions end with the rotation either way.
#SESSION_SECRET_PREVIOUS=

# Admin Credentials -- NO LONGER REQUIRED.
#
# The first administrator is created in the browser on first run, which also
# proves the password works before any other configuration is entered. Leave
# these unset for a new install.
#
# They still work, for a deployment that predates the setup flow or automates
# its provisioning: setting BOTH seeds an admin at startup and skips the setup
# flow entirely. The password must be 12+ characters with uppercase, lowercase,
# numbers, and special characters -- the same policy the setup form enforces.
# They are applied when the admin is created and again only when either value
# changes, so a password changed in the UI survives restarts. A changed value
# ends the admin's sessions and re-enables the account: the recovery path.
#ADMIN_USERNAME=admin
#ADMIN_PASSWORD=Your-Secure-P@ssw0rd-Here!

# --- Everything below this line until the ADVANCED section is a Settings page
# --- field. Set it here to seed the value, or leave it and use the app.

# Allow public email/password account registration. Disabled by default.
#AUTH_ALLOW_SELF_REGISTRATION=false

# Allow an OAuth sign-in to create a brand-new account for an unknown identity.
# Disabled by default: with OAuth enabled, existing users can still sign in (and
# link), but a first-time OAuth identity will NOT be auto-provisioned unless this
# is set to true. Enable it if OAuth is your intended onboarding path.
#AUTH_ALLOW_OAUTH_REGISTRATION=false

# Trust a configured OAuth IdP to set a new user's role/status from its profile
# claims (e.g. a `role` claim). Disabled by default: role/status are forced to
# safe values (user/active) for OAuth-created accounts regardless of IdP claims.
# Only enable this if you control the IdP and intend it to manage roles.
#AUTH_ALLOW_OAUTH_ROLE_FROM_CLAIMS=false

# OIDC-only mode: no local accounts at all. Disables credential sign-in, local
# account creation, password management, and the bootstrap admin seed, so
# ADMIN_USERNAME/ADMIN_PASSWORD above are not required. Enable this only once
# OAuth sign-in is confirmed working, or you will lock yourself out.
# This is also a Settings field; if you did lock yourself out with it, see
# SETTINGS_ENV_OVERRIDE below.
#AUTH_DISABLE_LOCAL_USERS=false

# The way back from a saved setting that locks you out. Names the variables that
# override a stored value instead of only filling in for a missing one - a space
# or comma separated list. Settings shows a field it covers greyed out, and stops
# offering to change it, until the variable is taken off this list.
#
# Worth having for AUTH_DISABLE_LOCAL_USERS (OIDC-only with OAuth not yet working
# leaves nobody able to sign in) and BASE_URL (a public URL that no longer matches
# the registered redirect URI breaks the OAuth round trip that would let you back
# in). It works for any variable in this file that has a Settings field.
#SETTINGS_ENV_OVERRIDE=AUTH_DISABLE_LOCAL_USERS,BASE_URL

# Trust the Host header when constructing URLs. Only enable this behind a proxy
# that rewrites Host, and only when BASE_URL cannot cover your setup.
#AUTH_TRUST_HOST=false

# Better Auth request rate limiting (separate from the LOGIN_* login throttle
# further down). Enabled by default.
#AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=true
#AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW=60
#AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=5

# Force a password reset for anyone still on a pre-argon2id bcrypt hash.
# When unset, the Settings -> Security toggle decides. Setting it here pins the
# policy and locks that toggle.
#AUTH_REQUIRE_PASSWORD_CHANGE_ON_LEGACY_HASH=true

# =============================================================================
# APPLICATION CONFIGURATION
# =============================================================================

# Public base URL for the application (IMPORTANT!)
# This is the URL where users access your Caddy Proxy Manager interface.
#
# ** REQUIRED FOR OAUTH: If using OAuth2/OIDC authentication, this MUST match
#    the redirect URI configured in your OAuth provider exactly.
#    The redirect URI is {BASE_URL}/api/auth/callback/{provider-id}, and the
#    exact value is shown in Settings -> OAuth Providers once the provider is
#    synced. Do not guess it.
#
# On first run this is filled in from the address you reached the setup page at,
# so it usually needs no value here at all.
#
# Examples:
#   - Local development: http://localhost:3000
#   - Production with domain: https://caddy-manager.example.com
#   - Production with IP: http://192.168.1.100:3000
#
# IMPORTANT: Do not include a trailing slash
#BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Display name in the sidebar, on the login card, and as the suffix on every
# page title. Defaults to "Caddy Proxy Manager".
#APP_NAME=Caddy Proxy Manager

# Accent colour of the dashboard: pink, purple, blue, cyan, teal, green, orange
# or red. Settings -> General -> Branding picks it too. Defaults to pink.
#ACCENT_COLOR=pink

# Let user icons fall back to Gravatar, which the browser fetches directly from
# gravatar.com. Set false to keep every avatar lookup off the network and lock
# the Settings -> User Avatars toggle. When unset, that toggle decides.
#AVATAR_GRAVATAR=true

# Demo mode. Everything works and is saved, but no Caddy server is configured:
# admin calls go to an in-memory Caddy, a simulated agent reports builds and
# ports as done, and real agents are refused. With no Caddy running, no
# certificate is ordered and no DNS provider's records are touched. Not a
# Settings field, so a demo's visitors cannot switch it off.
#DEMO_MODE=false

# =============================================================================
# ROOTLESS OPERATION (OPTIONAL)
# =============================================================================

# User and Group IDs for running containers as non-root
# Set these to match your host user to avoid permission issues with volumes
# Find your UID/GID with: id -u / id -g
#
# Defaults:
#   - Web service: PUID=10001, PGID=10001
#   - Caddy service: PUID=10000, PGID=10000
#
# For matching your host user (recommended for development):
#PUID=1000
#PGID=1000

# Caddy's GID, added to the web and agent containers' supplementary groups so they
# can use Caddy's logs. Must match Caddy's PGID above.
#CADDY_GID=10000

# The agent's own user and group. Separate from PUID/PGID, which already mean two
# different users to web and caddy.
#AGENT_PUID=10002
#AGENT_PGID=10002

# The controller's GID, added to the agent's supplementary groups so it can read the
# bootstrap token the controller leaves for it. Must match web's PGID above.
#CONTROLLER_GID=10001

# Domain this dashboard is served on.
#
# Two consumers. The bundled Caddyfile answers on it alongside http://localhost until CPM applies
# its own configuration. And setup reads it once, to turn on the managed host under
# Settings -> Dashboard Host that reverse-proxies this dashboard through the Caddy CPM manages -
# so a fresh install is already proxying something, and the thing it proxies is itself.
#
# After setup the stored setting is what that route is built from; changing this line then does
# nothing but move the Caddyfile's placeholder. Left unset, setup falls back to the hostname in
# BASE_URL, and the host is left off if that is a localhost address.
#
# You are never locked out by it: the controller publishes port 3000, so http://<host>:3000
# reaches the settings page that turns it off.
#DASHBOARD_DOMAIN=cpm.example.com

# =============================================================================
# OAUTH2/OIDC AUTHENTICATION (OPTIONAL)
# =============================================================================

# OAuth2/OIDC Provider (works with Authentik, Authelia, Keycloak, etc.)
# Enable OAuth2 authentication with any OIDC-compliant provider.
#
# Unlike the settings above, these are not Settings page fields: they are synced
# into the oauth_providers table at startup, so a provider has one source of
# truth. Providers added in the UI live only in that table. Either way works;
# the UI is easier, because it shows you the exact callback URL to register.
# All of these can be entered on the setup page instead, under "Identity provider" -- and are
# filled in from here when they are set, so saving that step moves them into the database and the
# variables below can then be deleted. They are also editable afterwards under Settings -> OAuth
# Providers, which is the only place to add a second provider.
#OAUTH_ENABLED=false
# Display name (e.g., "Authentik", "Keycloak")
#OAUTH_PROVIDER_NAME=OAuth2
#OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
#OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
# OIDC discovery URL (e.g., https://auth.example.com/application/o/app/)
#OAUTH_ISSUER=

# Optional: Override auto-discovered URLs (only if OIDC discovery doesn't work)
#OAUTH_AUTHORIZATION_URL=
#OAUTH_TOKEN_URL=
#OAUTH_USERINFO_URL=

# OAuth Settings
# Let a sign-in through the provider attach to the existing user with the same
# email. Off, that sign-in is refused and the user links the provider from their
# profile instead - manual linking works either way.
#OAUTH_ALLOW_AUTO_LINKING=false

# Scopes requested from the provider. Defaults to "openid email profile"; group
# claims usually need an extra scope (e.g. "openid email profile groups").
#OAUTH_SCOPES=openid email profile

# --- Group-based roles -------------------------------------------------------
# Assign CPM roles from the provider's group claim. Either name each role's
# groups explicitly (comma-separated, any one grants the role) or set a prefix
# and let <prefix>Admin / <prefix>Operator / <prefix>User / <prefix>Viewer be
# derived from it.
# Explicit group names take precedence over the prefix.
#OAUTH_ROLE_MAPPING=false
# Dots address nested claims: resource_access.cpm.roles
#OAUTH_GROUPS_CLAIM=groups
#OAUTH_GROUP_PREFIX=CPM_
#OAUTH_ADMIN_GROUP=
# Manages only the hosts and agents their CPM groups were granted
#OAUTH_OPERATOR_GROUP=
#OAUTH_USER_GROUP=
#OAUTH_VIEWER_GROUP=
# Role assigned when no role group matches: admin, operator, user or viewer.
# Anything else counts as user.
#OAUTH_DEFAULT_ROLE=user
# Mirror the remaining prefixed IdP groups into CPM groups
#OAUTH_SYNC_GROUPS=false

# OIDC back-channel logout is not configured here -- it is a URL you register at the identity
# provider, not a setting CPM reads. Point the provider's back-channel logout URL at
# {BASE_URL}/api/auth/oidc/backchannel-logout and CPM will end a user's sessions, and their
# forward-auth sessions, when the provider ends theirs. See the README.

# Example for Authentik:
#OAUTH_ENABLED=true
#OAUTH_PROVIDER_NAME=Authentik
#OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=your-client-id
#OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=your-client-secret
#OAUTH_ISSUER=https://auth.example.com/application/o/caddy-proxy/
#
# IMPORTANT: Configure the redirect URI in your OAuth provider:
#   Redirect URI = {BASE_URL}/api/auth/callback/{provider-id}
#   The provider id is derived from OAUTH_PROVIDER_NAME and is not guessable by
#   hand -- read the exact URL from Settings -> OAuth Providers after startup.
#   Example: https://caddy-manager.example.com/api/auth/callback/authentik-QXV0aG

# =============================================================================
# OPTIONAL: ADVANCED CONFIGURATION
# =============================================================================

# The database is configured from the POSTGRES_* variables above, which the app reads as discrete
# fields. That is deliberate: a password only has to be encoded when it goes into a URL, and a `/`
# or `@` in one silently reparses into a different host. As fields, any password works as typed.
#
# Point at a server other than the bundled `postgres` service with these:
#POSTGRES_HOST=postgres
#POSTGRES_PORT=5432
#POSTGRES_SSL=false

# Overrides all of the above when set. Only needed for something a URL can express and the fields
# cannot -- an sslmode beyond on/off, a search_path, a libpq connection option. A password in here
# must be percent-encoded (`/` is %2F, `@` is %40).
#DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@host:5432/database
#
# Or a file, to use SQLite instead: no server, one small instance. /app/data is the
# controller's volume. Never the pre-3.0 file itself -- the app migrates that one.
#DATABASE_URL=file:/app/data/cpm.db

# Connections the database pool may open. Requests beyond this queue, so raise it for a busy
# instance -- and keep the server's own max_connections above the total across all instances.
#DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10

# Caddy Admin API endpoint (usually no need to change). `caddy-admin` is Caddy's name on the
# internal network only web and the agent share with it. CADDY_ADMIN_LISTEN, the address Caddy
# binds there, is fixed in docker-compose.yml rather than read from this file.
#CADDY_API_URL=http://caddy-admin:2019

# Re-apply this controller's configuration to a Caddy that drifted away from it. A Settings
# field (Settings -> Agent); this is the first-start default. Turn it off only on a controller
# pointed at a Caddy it does not own, or the two monitors fight over it.
#CADDY_MONITOR_ENABLED=true

# Certificate storage directory (usually no need to change)
#CERTS_DIRECTORY=./data/certs

# Login rate limiting (optional, for custom rate limit settings)
#LOGIN_MAX_ATTEMPTS=5
#LOGIN_WINDOW_MS=300000
#LOGIN_BLOCK_MS=900000

# Per-account lock, whatever address the guesses come from: after the free
# failures each one locks the account, doubling from the first lock up to the
# longest. Settings fields (Settings -> Authentication -> Sign-in).
#ACCOUNT_LOCK_ENABLED=true
#ACCOUNT_LOCK_FREE_FAILURES=5
#ACCOUNT_LOCK_BASE_DELAY_MS=1000
#ACCOUNT_LOCK_MAX_DELAY_MS=900000

# Disable an account outright after this many failed sign-ins (same counter),
# until an administrator enables it. Off by default: anyone who knows a username
# can then disable that account. The last active administrator is only locked.
#ACCOUNT_LOCK_DISABLE_ENABLED=false
#ACCOUNT_LOCK_DISABLE_AFTER=10

# Non-default ports CPM forward-auth sites are served on, comma-separated. Caddy
# matches a host whatever the port, so a sign-in on any other port is refused.
# A Settings field (Settings -> Forward Auth); this is the first-start default.
#FORWARD_AUTH_ALLOWED_PORTS=

# Send X-CPM-User-Id as the sequential account number rather than a UUID.
# Installs upgraded from before the UUID are switched on by migration, since
# their upstreams already keyed accounts on the number.
# A Settings field (Settings -> Forward Auth); this is the first-start default.
#FORWARD_AUTH_SEQUENTIAL_USER_IDS=false

# How long the agent waits for a Caddy image rebuild before giving up, in
# seconds. Rebuilds happen when you change the module selection in
# Settings -> Caddy Build; xcaddy compiles Caddy from source, so a few minutes
# is normal and slow or ARM hosts can take considerably longer.
#CADDY_BUILD_TIMEOUT=1800

# Build Caddy's image yourself instead of letting the agent do it, so the agent
# needs no Docker build access (set GRPC and SESSION to 0 on docker-socket-proxy).
# Settings -> Caddy Build then shows the docker build command for your module
# selection, and a button that loads the image once you have built it.
# CADDY_IMAGE is the tag Caddy runs: not the shipped name, or loading would pull
# the registry's copy over yours. Run `docker compose up -d agent` after setting it.
#CADDY_BUILD_MODE=external
#CADDY_IMAGE=caddy-proxy-manager-caddy:custom

# A host directory the agent may read certificates from, such as certbot's
# /etc/letsencrypt: Certificates -> Import -> From a file on an agent. The agent
# reads it in a throwaway container that mounts only this directory, read-only,
# and sends the PEM to the controller; Caddy is never pointed at the files. The
# path is the one the Docker daemon sees. Unset, the feature is off.
#CERT_FILES_HOST_DIR=/etc/letsencrypt

# Dial address Caddy uses to reach this app internally for forward_auth.
# Derived from the container network by default; override only if Caddy cannot
# reach the web service at the derived address.
#FORWARD_AUTH_INTERNAL_URL=

# Cutoff after which secrets still encrypted with the legacy key are refused,
# forcing re-encryption. ISO 8601 date, or "never" to disable the cutoff.
#LEGACY_KEY_CUTOFF_DATE=never

# Container paths, only relevant to non-Docker deployments where these
# directories do not exist.
#ACME_CA_ROOT_DIR=/acme-ca
#L4_PORTS_DIR=/app/data

# Pin which pre-3.0 SQLite database the migration flow offers, instead of
# scanning the usual locations for one. Only read while setup is unfinished.
#LEGACY_SQLITE_PATH=/legacy/caddy-proxy-manager.db

# Where the agent dials to reach its controller. The agent connects out and holds an event stream
# open; the controller never dials the agent, so no inbound port is needed on the agent's host.
# The compose default points at the controller in this same stack. An agent on another host needs
# the controller's reachable address, and is paired from that host with:
#   cpm-agent --pair --host <controller address> --code <code from Settings -> Agents>
#
# Over a tailnet this is just an address: a Tailscale/Headscale IP, a MagicDNS name, or an
# https:// name published with `tailscale serve`. An https:// address with no port means 443.
#   CONTROLLER_URL=http://100.98.59.37:3000
#   CONTROLLER_URL=http://cpm-controller:3000
#   CONTROLLER_URL=https://cpm-controller.tailnet-1234.ts.net
#CONTROLLER_URL=http://web:3000

# A bare host in CONTROLLER_URL or --host means https://, except loopback and single-label names
# like `web`. Plain http:// to an address that is not private (loopback, a compose name, RFC 1918,
# a tailnet) is refused, because pairing sends the shared secret over it. Set this to accept that.
#CONTROLLER_ALLOW_INSECURE_HTTP=false

# A CA bundle the agent should trust in addition to the system store, for a controller behind TLS
# issued by a private CA. Mount it into the agent container and point this at the path.
#NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS=/certs/internal-ca.crt

# Pair on first start instead of idling until `cpm-agent --pair` is run. Generated under
# Settings -> Agents, valid five minutes, single use.
#PAIRING_CODE=

# A label the agent reports to the controller. Neither value opens a network listener.
#AGENT_MODE=standalone

# Listen address and health-check target for the cpm-server binary. Only used
# when running the compiled executable directly instead of the container.
#
# HOST defaults to "::", a dual-stack socket that accepts IPv4 as well. Set
# 0.0.0.0 to bind IPv4 only.
#PORT=3000
#HOST=::
#CPM_APP_ROOT=
#CPM_HEALTHCHECK_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/health

# =============================================================================
# UPDATE CHECKING
# =============================================================================

# Whether to ask the registry, a few times a day, if a newer release has been
# published. The only request this app makes to the internet on its own.
# Also a Settings field; a value saved there wins over this.
#UPDATE_CHECK_ENABLED=true

# The image namespace this deployment pulls from, without the image name. Change
# it if you run a fork, or the check reports releases you cannot pull.
#UPDATE_IMAGE_REPOSITORY=ghcr.io/silentspud/caddy-proxy-manager

# =============================================================================
# DOCKER COMPOSE PROFILES
# =============================================================================

# Comma-separated list of profiles to activate.
# Available profiles:
#   clickhouse   - ClickHouse analytics database (traffic + WAF events, 30-day TTL)
#
# Only needed when you have no agent. With one running - which the bundled
# compose file does - Settings → Analytics starts and stops ClickHouse for you,
# profile or not, and this can be left empty.
#
# Pick one owner. Once the credential lives in Settings, remove the profile here
# and the variable below: leaving both means your own `docker compose up -d`
# also creates the container, from the now-stale copy.
#
# Left empty because this compose file bundles an agent, which makes Settings the
# owner. Set it only if you run without one.
#COMPOSE_PROFILES=clickhouse

# =============================================================================
# CLICKHOUSE ANALYTICS
# =============================================================================

# Everything below is a Settings → Analytics field. A value saved there is
# stored in the database and wins over the variable, which can then be deleted.
# These remain for a first start, and for a deployment with no agent: the agent
# passes the saved credentials to Compose itself, so it does not need them here.
#
# Whether analytics run at all. Leave unset to decide from whether a password is
# set below, which is what earlier releases did.
#ANALYTICS_ENABLED=true

# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32. Required - ClickHouse will not start
# without one.
#
# The controller pushes these credentials to every agent, which is how each host
# writes its own Caddy log events. Nothing to configure on the agent side.
#
# Prefer Settings → Analytics: saved there it is encrypted at rest, and the agent
# hands it to `docker compose`
# in the environment of the command it runs rather than leaving a plaintext copy
# on the host. Left commented so a fresh install cannot end up provisioning
# ClickHouse with the placeholder that used to sit here. Uncomment it only
# alongside the COMPOSE_PROFILES above, on a deployment with no agent.
#CLICKHOUSE_PASSWORD=
#CLICKHOUSE_URL=http://clickhouse:8123
#CLICKHOUSE_USER=cpm
#CLICKHOUSE_DB=analytics

# How long analytics events (traffic + WAF) are retained before ClickHouse's
# TTL deletes them. Defaults to 30 days. Lower this to use less disk space.
# Changing it migrates the existing tables' TTL on the next startup.
#CLICKHOUSE_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# =============================================================================
# GEOIP (OPTIONAL)
# =============================================================================

# MaxMind GeoLite2 databases - needed for geo blocking. The controller downloads
# them itself and checks MaxMind for updates once a day by default. Agents on other
# hosts fetch them through it, with no licence key of their own.
# Get credentials at: https://www.maxmind.com/en/geolite2/signup
#
# Prefer Settings → GeoIP, where the licence key is encrypted at rest. Set here
# it is a plaintext copy on the host for as long as the file exists -- and the
# same key authenticates MaxMind's billable web services, not only the free
# GeoLite2 downloads. Left commented for that reason: once saved in Settings,
# delete the lines.
#
# Whether GeoIP is used at all. Leave unset to decide from whether the databases
# are already present, which is what earlier releases did.
#GEOIP_ENABLED=true
#GEOIPUPDATE_ACCOUNT_ID=
#GEOIPUPDATE_LICENSE_KEY=

# Hours between checks for newer databases, 1-168. MaxMind rebuilds GeoLite ASN
# daily, and Country and City on Tuesdays and Fridays; a check downloads only
# what changed.
#GEOIP_UPDATE_INTERVAL_HOURS=24

# =============================================================================
# EMAIL (OPTIONAL)
# =============================================================================

# An SMTP server for password reset links, user invitations and certificate
# expiry alerts. Without one the sign-in page offers no password reset.
#
# Prefer Settings → Email, where the password is encrypted at rest and a test
# message can be sent. Set here, the password is a plaintext copy on the host.
#
# Whether email is on. Leave unset to turn it on whenever SMTP_HOST is set.
#SMTP_ENABLED=true
#SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com
# starttls (usually port 587), tls (usually 465) or none (trusted relays only).
#SMTP_SECURITY=starttls
#SMTP_PORT=587
# Leave both empty for a relay that accepts mail without signing in.
#SMTP_USERNAME=
#SMTP_PASSWORD=
# The From address; the application name is shown as the sender's name.
#SMTP_FROM=proxy@example.com

# Comma-separated addresses for certificate alerts (empty: every active
# administrator), and extra recipients of every notification.
#EMAIL_ALERT_RECIPIENTS=
# Alert once a certificate has fewer days than this left, 0-90. Caddy renews
# well before then, so an alert means renewal is failing. 0 turns alerts off.
#CERTIFICATE_EXPIRY_ALERT_DAYS=14

# Admin notifications, emailed to the recipients above (or every active
# administrator) in one message per minute. Settings fields (Settings -> Email
# -> Notifications); these are first-start defaults.
#NOTIFY_ACCOUNT_DISABLED=true
#NOTIFY_ADMIN_LOCKED=true
#NOTIFY_ADMIN_ADDED=true
#NOTIFY_AGENT_OFFLINE=true
#NOTIFY_AGENT_OFFLINE_MINUTES=5
#NOTIFY_UPSTREAM_ERRORS=true
#NOTIFY_UPSTREAM_ERROR_COUNT=10
#NOTIFY_UPSTREAM_ERROR_MINUTES=5
#NOTIFY_CADDY_APPLY=true
